            <ArticleTitle>Responding to concerning posts on social media: Insights and solutions from American Indian and Alaska Native youth.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>63-87</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5820/aian.2403.2017.63</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This study aimed to understand American Indian and Alaska Native (AI/AN) adolescents' perspectives on concerning social media posts, including those expressing suicidal intent.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A purposive sample of AI/AN youth were recruited; trained facilitators conducted focus groups. Participants discussed experiences viewing concerning posts on social media. Qualitative analysis used the constant comparative method.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 32 AI/AN youth ages 14-22 participated. Three salient themes emerged: 1) youth typically respond alone; 2) barriers to action; 3) recommended training and support - trusted adults, anonymity, and privacy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Study findings will guide the development of resources trusted by AI/AN youth.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
